// The Appeal, ch11: The Hearing. Thursday, August 19 to Tuesday, September 14, 2027 (Federal on her birthday; Teo's carport that night; Federal on Tuesday, August 24; Teo's kitchen on Sunday, August 29; Federal on Thursday, September 9; the call from Brighton and Federal on Sunday, September 12; two days at the Adams County Justice Center, Monday and Tuesday, September 13 and 14).
// Coverage per the bible: scenes LR08 LR07 LR19 LR20 LR06 LR02 LR13 LR15 RC14x2 RC10 RC13; quiz LR08x2 LR07x2 LR19 LR20 LR06 LR02 LR13 LR15 LR18 LR04 RC14x2 RC10x2 RC13 RC04.
// Full-length passage (p2): a comparative pair on eyewitness memory (psychology), written fresh. Passage A from Loftus and Palmer, "Reconstruction of Automobile Destruction," Journal of Verbal Learning and Verbal Behavior 13 (1974), 585 to 589, read 2026-10-17 from the PDF: University of Washington; Experiment I, 45 students, seven films, verbs smashed, collided, bumped, hit, contacted, mean estimates 40.8, 39.3, 38.1, 34.0, 31.8 mph; four films were staged crashes at 20, 30, 40 and 40 mph; the actual speed controlled little variance and the phrasing considerable variance; Experiment II, 150 students, a film under a minute with a four second accident, 50 smashed, 50 hit, 50 not asked about speed; a week later "Did you see any broken glass?" (none in the film): yes 16 of 50, 7 of 50, 6 of 50; the authors propose that information from the event and information supplied afterward are integrated into one memory. Passage B from Wixted and Wells, "The Relationship Between Eyewitness Confidence and Identification Accuracy: A New Synthesis," Psychological Science in the Public Interest 18(1) (2017), 10 to 65, read 2026-10-17 from the PubMed abstract (PMID 28395650): the U.S. legal system increasingly treats confidence as uninformative, reasonable once because of questionable procedures; pristine conditions are an initial, uncontaminated test, a fair lineup, no administrator influence, an immediate confidence statement; under them, mock crime and police field studies show for adults that confidence and accuracy are strongly related and high confidence identifications are very accurate; unfair lineups seriously compromise even high confidence identifications; initial low confidence identifications are error prone, which helps exonerate the innocent. The passage's last clause (their review doesn't vouch for confidence built up before trial) is a reading of the abstract's "initial" limit, kept soft.
// Short case passage (p1): the Colorado Bureau of Investigation DNA report summary, Thursday, August 19, 2027 (new, fiction). Victim's own DNA appears in his scrapings, as it would.
// Law and practice, story level only. The ch6 header's citations stand: 18-1-416(1.5) wants a hearing within thirty days of a favorable result, and Monday, September 13 is day twenty five after August 19; results go to the state and national databases (ch8). Ana's card from ch6 (NEW. ALL FOUR.) carries the standard; the ch6 calls make Teo and the boy fail line two because Luis knew, and Ana concedes it (f11s8). Luis is moved to the county jail in Brighton for the hearing (inferred: public listings read 2026-10-17 put the Adams County Detention Facility in Brighton, with outbound calls through a facility calling service; the transport itself isn't verified and the page says only that he was brought down). No sequestration order is mentioned. Teo's legal exposure is never stated; he has his own lawyer, Celeste Abeyta (new; name checked clean across the repo, the bibles and the canon, 2026-10-17).
// Weekdays computed 2026-10-17: Thursday, August 19; Tuesday, August 24; Wednesday, August 25; Sunday, August 29; Saturday, September 4 (Lupe turns seven); Thursday, September 9; Friday, September 10; Sunday, September 12; Monday, September 13; Tuesday, September 14, 2027. Friday, July 16, 2027. Sunday, October 28, 2007.
// Reveal schedule: the DNA result on her birthday, nobody remarks on it (opening, f11s1): Luis under the right hand, an unknown man under the left, Teo excluded, no database match; the one neck echo (f11s1). Teo's own lawyer, the name Ana wrote him in July (f11s3). Lupe is told on Sunday, August 29 (f11s4), the promise from ch6 kept; she turns seven on the fourth (f11s5). Dani won't coach Teo (f11s4). Her move is the call the night before (f11s6 and f11s7): Teo testifies whatever Luis says, and only Luis can say why the boy swung; Luis answers her knight with his own. Ana's circled 1 was written the week after the 2010 opinion, and the girl with the car keys was Dani (f11s8). Meredith asks Ana why once, in the hall (f11s8). Three testimonies on the page: Teo (f11s9), Consuelo (f11s11), Luis (f11s12); Solberg (f11s10) and Gene (f11s11, his one line: boy? at nine, excuse me, son at three) in a paragraph each; Dani recalled (f11s11). Kept for ch12: Phil's note, the car, the last move, Consuelo and the order, "Then who?", the envelope's top line uncrossed. Luis is never called smaller. "Most dangerous sentence" is held.
// Flags set here: none. Read here: keptteosecret (f11s3), wroteshannon (f11s8), ledconsuelo and calledgenealiar (f11s11), overclaimed (f11s12). The ch10 pick at f10s8 (index 1, TEO'S WORD on the card) is read in f11s6. This chapter's f11s4 pick (index 2, the shoes promise) is read in f11s9. Review fixes applied 2026-10-17 (lsat_ch11_fixes.md): Teo's phone line replaces the ch6 best-only promise, Dani's recall matches the ch1 transcript (The cycle was done), Ana's chair from her division office is canon.
