(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).lsat=window.HALCYON_DATA.lsat||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.lsat.full||[]).push({
  num: 11, part: `Part Three: The Truth`, title: `The Hearing`, weeks: `August to September 2027`,
  tasks: [`LR02`,`LR04`,`LR06`,`LR07`,`LR08`,`LR13`,`LR15`,`LR18`,`LR19`,`LR20`,`RC04`,`RC10`,`RC13`,`RC14`],
  cast: [
    [`Teo Ruiz`,`Dani's brother, 30. Told Ana in August he'll testify. His father has said no, twice.`],
    [`Ana Brennan`,`Appellate lawyer on Federal Boulevard. Luis's lawyer, and not Teo's.`],
    [`Luis Ruiz`,`Dani's father, 62. Calls Wednesdays at seven. Owes her a move: her knight is in the middle of the board.`],
    [`Meredith Sloane`,`Chief deputy district attorney in Adams County. Agreed to the testing in May and opposes everything else.`],
    [`Consuelo Baca`,`Saw a man in a red jacket and a ball cap from her kitchen window in 2007. Never saw a face.`],
    [`Shannon Hensley`,`Travis Hensley's younger sister. She took his call at 11:04 that night.`],
    [`Judge Martin Esquibel`,`Adams County District Court. Few words, all of them careful.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `Thursday, August 19, she was thirty five. Nobody at the office knew it, and she didn't tell anybody. She ate lunch at her desk over somebody else's motion to suppress, half a turkey sandwich and an apple, and at ten after two her phone buzzed against the stapler with Ana's name on it.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"It came,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"I'm not reading it to you over the phone. Come when you're done there."</span>`,
    `She put the other half of the sandwich in her drawer, told Joel she was taking the afternoon, and caught the 2:31 out Colfax. A man across the aisle was eating sunflower seeds out of a paper cup and spitting the shells back into it, one at a time, the whole way.`,
    `The laundromat was half empty in the middle of the afternoon, and the stairs smelled like it anyway. Upstairs Ana had two stapled pages face down on the desk with her hand flat on top of them, like they might get up and leave.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Ana's Pencil: everything at once`,
    sections: [
      { h: `The only one who saw it`,
        body: [`September on Federal. One dryer's going downstairs and the rest have quit for the night. Dicta's on the rug with her nose in her own tail. Pencil. Tonight is a little of everything, because a hearing is a little of everything, and so is the last month before any test.`,
          `Start with a witness. An argument that rests on one person's word has a soft spot built into it, and the test will ask you to find it or to cover it. To weaken it, look for a reason the witness would say the same thing whether it happened or not. Interest is the usual one. A man selling his house swears the roof doesn't leak. He'd swear it in a downpour. That doesn't make him wrong. It means his swearing doesn't tell you much.`,
          `To strengthen it, look for the other kind of fact: something the witness did or said that only makes sense if it's true. A roofer who tells you the leak is his own fault, and loses the job for saying it, is worth listening to. So is a record made before anybody knew what it would show. Evidence that was fixed in place before the fight started can't have been bent by the fight.`,
          `Keep the two moves apart. Showing that a witness has a reason to lie weakens an argument that leans on him. It doesn't prove he's lying. If an answer gets from "he has a reason" to "so it's false," it's gone too far, and you'll see that move again tonight as a flaw.`],
        terms: [[`Interested witness`,`Someone who gains or loses depending on what's believed. Interest is a reason to check the testimony, not proof that it's false.`],[`Corroboration`,`Evidence from another source that agrees with a witness on a point that matters.`]],
        exam: `Stems ask what most weakens or most strengthens the argument. The right weakener gives the testimony a reason to exist even if it weren't true. The right strengthener closes that door.` },
      { h: `Where they part`,
        body: [`Two speakers, and the question asks what they disagree about. The rule that saves you: both of them have to have said something about it. One says yes and the other says no. If only one of them touched the point, it isn't the point at issue, however much it sounds like one.`,
          `So test each answer twice. Would the first speaker say yes or no? Would the second? You want one of each. A point of agreement runs the same test and wants two yeses. People lose these by picking what the two are fighting about in the larger sense, the case or the policy, when the stimulus only lets you say what they dispute in these sentences.`,
          `Try it on two neighbors. One says the old elm should come down before it drops a limb on the garage. The other says the elm is sound, and that if anything comes down it should be the dead maple by the fence. They disagree about the elm. You can't say they disagree about the maple, because the first neighbor never mentioned it. And you can't say they agree the garage matters, because the second one never said so either.`,
          `A concession is a gift here. When one speaker says the other is right about something, you've found a point of agreement in the speaker's own words.`],
        terms: [[`Point at issue`,`A claim one speaker affirms and the other denies.`],[`Point of agreement`,`A claim both speakers are committed to.`]],
        exam: `Stems ask on which point the two disagree, or what both are committed to accepting. Check every choice against both speakers before you keep it.` },
      { h: `Same mistake, and what a line is for`,
        body: [`Parallel flaw, which you met in April. Name the mistake in plain words before you look at the choices: throws out what somebody says because of who's saying it. Then find the choice that makes the same mistake about anything at all, a used car or a weather report or a restaurant review. The subject doesn't matter. The move does.`,
          `Two traps. One has the same subject and a different move. The other makes a milder claim. "His interest means we should check what he says" isn't the same mistake as "his interest means he's lying." The first one isn't a mistake at all.`,
          `Role questions ask what a sentence is doing. Find the conclusion, then ask whether the sentence holds it up, explains something, or answers somebody. A fact can hurt your side and still be doing work for you, if it explains away what the other side built on.`],
        terms: [[`Role`,`What a statement does in an argument: premise, conclusion, intermediate conclusion, concession or background.`]],
        exam: `Parallel flaw stems ask which argument is most similar in its flawed reasoning. Role stems ask which choice most accurately describes the role a claim plays.` },
      { h: `Two passages`,
        body: [`A Reading Comprehension section has four sets, and one of them may pair two shorter passages on one subject. LSAC says a section has one of these or none. Read A all the way through and say its point in a sentence. Then read B with one question in mind: what does B do with A's point? Agree and add to it. Disagree. Accept it and put a limit on it. Answer something A left open.`,
          `The questions follow from that. How the passages relate. What both authors would accept. What one would say about the other. Which situation is like something in one of them. For that last kind, strip the case down to its bones the way you would for a parallel question. If words changed a memory, look for a choice where words change a memory, not one that happens to be about cars.`,
          `Say passage A reports that honeybees find their way home by the sun, and passage B describes bees on overcast days that still find home by landmarks they learned on their first flights. B hasn't refuted A. It has accepted A and found the edge of it. A question about how they relate wants that word, edge or limit or qualification, and the trap answer calls it a rebuttal.`,
          `The usual wrong answer on a relation question says B rejects A when B only narrows it. A writer who says that's true, but not always, has agreed with you on most of it.`],
        terms: [[`Comparative passages`,`A pair of shorter passages on related subjects, with questions about each and about how they relate.`]],
        exam: `Stems ask which most accurately describes the relationship between the passages, which statement both authors would most likely accept, and which is most analogous to a situation described in one of them.` },
      { h: `A rule, all the way through`,
        body: [`Last, a skill you've used since March. Apply a principle by walking the case through every condition it names. Not most of them. Every one. If a case fails one condition, the rule doesn't reach it, however well it does on the others. And don't add a condition the rule never mentioned, an age or a date or a feeling, to make a hard case come out the way you'd like. You've been doing it all summer on my card. Do it once more on the test.`],
        terms: [[`Conforms`,`Meets every condition the principle states, with nothing added.`]],
        exam: `Stems ask which judgment most closely conforms to the principle. The right answer meets every condition, and nothing else is needed.` }
    ],
    video: [`NFSscJ_-1cE`,`LSAT Reading Comprehension: Comparative Passages | LSAT Reading Comprehension Tips`,`Blueprint LSAT Preparation`,`4:58`]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f11s1`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC13`, title:`Left hand`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The report was two pages with the state seal at the top and a case number she knew by heart. Ana turned it over and slid it across the desk, and then she got up and went to the window and looked down at the bus bench, so she wouldn't be watching Dani read.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Colorado Bureau of Investigation, forensic DNA report, summary of results">Item 7. Fingernail scrapings and clippings, right and left hands of Travis Hensley, collected at autopsy, October 28, 2007.<br>Reference samples compared: Travis Hensley (blood standard, 2007). Luis Ruiz (2027). Teo Ruiz (collected July 16, 2027).<br>Right hand. A mixture of DNA from two people. Travis Hensley is included as a contributor. Luis Ruiz is included as a contributor. Teo Ruiz is excluded.<br>Left hand. A mixture of DNA from two people. Travis Hensley is included as a contributor. The other contributor is male and is designated Unknown Male 1. Luis Ruiz and Teo Ruiz are excluded as the source of Unknown Male 1.<br>The profile of Unknown Male 1 was searched in the state and national DNA databases. No match was found.<br>These results identify whose DNA is present. They do not establish when, or by what kind of contact, any of it was deposited.</prop>`,
        `She read it twice. Right hand, her father. She'd told Ana in May that his would be under there, and why, standing at this desk with her bag still on her shoulder. I saw his neck that Sunday. Now it had a state seal over it and a staple in the corner.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"All those years on a shelf in a property room in Commerce City,"</span> Ana said to the window, <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"and it comes back with something on both hands. Pick me the line I carry into that courtroom. I get one."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`The right hand. Pa's DNA, from the fight he told Gene he was never in.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"The State had that one in 2008 without any lab,"</span> Ana says. Ronnie saw the fight, and the jury heard all about it. It proves her father lied to Gene, and nobody in Brighton is going to argue about that anymore. It doesn't move the motion an inch.`,
          why:`Strengthen a passage claim. True, and it cuts the wrong way: it proves Luis lied about the fight, which the State already argued in 2008. It does nothing for the claim that somebody else was at the curb.`},
        {t:`Teo's excluded from both hands. Neither of the two profiles is his.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`Ana says that's good for her brother, and it shuts one door, since nobody gets to stand up in Brighton and say the second man was the boy. She'll give it a sentence. It isn't the line she meant.`,
          why:`Strengthen a passage claim. It helps a little, since it rules out Teo as the source of the second profile. But the motion turns on there being a second man at all, and that's the left hand.`},
        {t:`The left hand. A second man's DNA, and it isn't Pa's or Teo's.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:4},
          after:`Ana turns around from the window and says that's the one. She reads the left hand out loud to the room, and then she reads the last line too, the one about when and how, because Meredith will read it out loud if she doesn't. In 2008 the State told a jury that one man fought Travis Hensley that night and the man in red was him, and under his other hand there was somebody that jury never heard about. Ana writes LEFT HAND on an index card. The card goes in her shirt pocket, behind the pencil.`,
          why:`Strengthen a passage claim. The motion says the State's case broke when it assumed the man who fought Travis was the only man who had hold of him. A second male profile under his nails, excluded as Luis's and as Teo's, is new evidence of another man in contact with him. The report's last line limits it: it can't say when.`},
        {t:`No match anywhere. Whoever the other man is, he's never been caught.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Never been put in a database,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's all no match means. Most men in Colorado have never been swabbed for anything."</span> It tells them nothing about the curb, and it doesn't make a stranger into a suspect.`,
          why:`Strengthen a passage claim. A database miss says only that the profile isn't on file. It doesn't help the motion, and "never been caught" reads more into it than the report says.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s2`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC10`, title:`Excluded`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`She drove over to Teo's at nine without calling. Teo was out in the carport in a lawn chair, Sprite by his boot, string lights from March over his head not plugged in. Bianca's car was gone. The only light in the house was Lupe's turtle. She sat down on the side door step next to the charcoal and told him all of it. Pa under one hand, some other man under the other one, him under neither.`,
        `He listened with his elbows on his knees. When she was done he said excluded back to her, slow, the way he'd read a part number off a box, and then he said, <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"So it wasn't me. That's what that means, right? It wasn't me."</span>`,
        `She'd read that word about fifty times on the bus home, between Federal and her stop, with the report folded in half on top of the prep book. Whatever she told him it meant, he was going to keep.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"It's like a blood test that clears a man, Teo. You're cleared of every bit of it."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`He looks at her for a long time, and she watches him almost believe it. Then he says, <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"I never touched him with my hands, Dani. Just the flashlight."</span> He knows what she just did. A test of what was under a man's nails can't clear a boy who never got that close, and he's too good a mechanic not to see where the part doesn't fit.`,
          why:`Analogy. Too strong. Exclusion means Teo's DNA isn't in the scrapings. He never had hold of Travis, so that's the result you'd expect either way. It clears him of nothing.`},
        {t:`"It's like no prints on a glass, Teo. You didn't hold it. You were in the room."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:3,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"I never touched him with my hands,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Just the flashlight."</span> She says she knows. She says she knows, and that's what the glass is for. He turns the can around on his knee a few times. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"But somebody had hold of him. Some other guy."</span> She says somebody did. The paper doesn't say when. He nods at the dark yard for a while, and then he gets up and plugs the string lights in, which he hasn't done since March.`,
          why:`Analogy. Exclusion says his DNA isn't among what was under the nails. Like prints missing from a glass, it shows he didn't leave that trace. Whether he was in the room is a question the glass can't answer, and here only Teo's own word answers it.`},
        {t:`"It's like somebody else's prints on the bat, Teo. That's the man who swung."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"When?"</span> he says. She doesn't know when. The report doesn't know when. He asks whether the other guy was at the curb, and she has to say she doesn't know that either, and she watches him set down the thing she just handed him.`,
          why:`Analogy. It reads the unknown profile as the attacker. The report says a second man's DNA is present. It doesn't say when it got there or that he struck anybody.`},
        {t:`"It's like a smudge nobody can read, Teo. It doesn't tell anybody a thing."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`He frowns. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Then why'd Ana call you down there?"</span> Because it isn't a smudge. It says plainly whose DNA is there and whose isn't, and his isn't. She's made it smaller than it is to keep from making it bigger, and he can tell.`,
          why:`Analogy. Too weak. The result is clear about what it shows: Teo isn't a contributor. An unreadable smudge misses that it does tell you something, just less than Teo hoped.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s3`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR13`, title:`Not my client`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, August 24, there was a sticky note on the corner of Ana's desk in her own pencil. CELESTE ABEYTA, and a number under it.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Your brother's lawyer,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"She called me at eight this morning. It's the name I wrote him in July. He carried it around in his wallet for a month and then he called it."</span> She leaned back and the chair said so. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Your brother isn't my client. He never was. Now he's got somebody whose client he is, and that's the best thing that's happened in this case since May."</span>`,
        `Celeste had asked her one thing, Ana said, what Teo had to lose, and Ana had told her that was Celeste's to answer and not hers. Dani didn't ask what the answer was. As far as anybody would say out loud, it wasn't a cell. It was Pa, and keeping still since he was ten, and whatever a man has left after he says in a courtroom that he hit somebody in the head with a steel flashlight when he was ten.`,
        ((s.flags.keptteosecret||G.keptteosecret)
          ?`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"And from now to the thirteenth, whatever he says to you, I hear it the next morning,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Not the next Friday. You don't get to decide what I know."</span>`
          :`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"And from now to the thirteenth, whatever he says to you, I hear it the next morning,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Same as July. That part worked fine."</span>`),
        `She took the card out of her shirt pocket, the one she'd shown Dani in March with the four lines on it, gone soft at the corners from riding around against her pencil all summer. She set it on the desk between them and asked what it did with Teo on the witness stand, one line at a time, the way she'd have asked it of a stranger.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`All four. No jury heard him, and at ten he couldn't have told anybody.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Whose knowing does line two care about?"</span> Ana says. Him and his lawyer. Not the boy. Her father knew where his son was every minute of that night, because he drove him there. Ana waits until Dani says it out loud.`,
          why:`Apply the principle. Line two asks whether the defendant and his lawyer could have known it with reasonable care. Teo's age doesn't enter into it. Luis knew.`},
        {t:`Line two stops him. Pa knew, and the judge can't hear a word of it.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Who told you that?"</span> Ana says. The card is a test for what counts as new. It isn't a list of who gets to talk. Failing line two means he isn't the reason they're in court. It doesn't mean he waits in the hall.`,
          why:`Apply the principle. Half right. He fails line two, so his account isn't new evidence. But the card sorts evidence, it doesn't silence witnesses, and his testimony can still bear on what the DNA means.`},
        {t:`Line four. A son who says he hit the man gets Pa an acquittal.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Ana asks if he does, in a voice that doesn't want an answer. Four's the last line on the card, she says, and you only get to it by going through the other three, and her brother doesn't make it past two. Neither does anybody who skips to the line they like best.`,
          why:`Apply the principle. It jumps to the last condition. A principle applies only when every condition is met, and Teo fails line two first.`},
        {t:`Line two stops him. Pa knew. He can still help the judge read the DNA.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:4},
          after:`Ana says that's it. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"He isn't new. Meredith's going to say so before her coffee gets cold, and I'm going to stand up and agree with her."</span> What's new is under that left hand, she says, and Teo's how a judge reads it, why their father lied to Gene and why his skin's under the other hand and where the truck was at five after eleven. Then she writes TEO on the back, small.`,
          why:`Apply the principle. Walk Teo through all four lines. He fails line two, since Luis always knew, so his account can't be the new evidence. That doesn't keep him off the stand. The new evidence is the DNA, and his testimony bears on what it would mean to a jury.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s4`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR06`, title:`Which part`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Teo had called her that week, late, from the truck. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"She's seven on the fourth. We're telling her Sunday. You're coming."</span> He hung up before she could answer.`,
        `That Sunday, the twenty ninth, they told Lupe after lunch. Bianca kept a hand on the back of Lupe's chair. Teo had a work order in his shirt pocket with some of it written on the back, and he never took it out. Lupe chewed and listened and asked one thing, if he was the one who sent the knight, and Dani said he was. <span class="said" data-who="Lupe">"Can he come to my party?"</span> Teo said not this one, mija. She asked could she have his crusts.`,
        `Around four he walked Dani out to her car and stood there with his keys. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Help me with it,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Be her. Ask me how she's gonna ask it."</span>`,
        `It was all true, what he'd told her in July. A true thing doesn't get less true because you go over it twice with your sister in a driveway, so going over it couldn't hurt. She got that far, standing on his oil stain.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"Once through, then. It's all true, Teo. Practice can't change a true thing."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`They start under the carport light, her question, his answer, and the second time through he says fifteen feet where in July he'd said close. They both hear it. She stops right there. It was true in July. It's still true. It just isn't only his anymore.`,
          why:`Flaw. The argument goes from the story being true to rehearsing it being harmless. That treats what's true of the events as if it were true of the telling. A true memory can still pick up the questioner's words, and a witness drilled by his sister sounds drilled.`},
        {t:`"Ana said no. If she found out I'd helped you, she'd drop us both."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Ana,"</span> he says, and almost laughs. The right answer, for the wrong reason. She's said no on account of a rule and a lawyer, and he hears it as one more person in this family protecting the case from him. He gets in the truck.`,
          why:`Flaw. The right result for the wrong reason. It never answers the argument that true testimony can't be hurt by practice. It leans on Ana's say so, and Teo hears exactly that.`},
        {t:`"You told Ana why yourself. If I help, you won't know which part's yours."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:3,health:4},
          after:`He turns the keys over a couple more times. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Yeah,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"I did say that."</span> She tells him she'll be right behind the rail the whole time and she won't help him with one word of it, not one. He gets the truck door open and stands in it. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Then don't look at me up there."</span> She says she'll look at her shoes. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Those shoes,"</span> he says, and gets in, and backs the truck out so she can get out.`,
          why:`Flaw. The argument assumes that because the account is true, going over it can't change it. But practice changes what a witness says and how, and then nobody, him included, can tell which words came from the night and which came from the carport. A true story doesn't protect the telling.`},
        {t:`"Just keep it short up there. Short answers don't give her anything."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`It's the first thing a lawyer tells every witness, and she's just told it to her brother, and he nods like it's useful. It's coaching. Short or long, he'd be saying it the way she told him to, and on Monday he'd be thinking about her the whole time he said it.`,
          why:`Flaw. It drops the argument and then does the thing anyway. Advice on how to answer is the coaching itself, and it changes the testimony the same way practice would.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s5`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC14`, title:`Separate`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday, September 9, five days after Lupe turned seven in the paper crown from March, Ana had both reports out side by side on the desk, the 2007 autopsy and Solberg's letter, with a pencil line down the margin of each where the word separate came up.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Solberg's Monday afternoon,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Meredith's going to read her the 2007 report out loud and ask her if she agrees with it. Your brother's going to be sitting there when she answers. Put these next to each other."</span>`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Two lines, side by side">2007: The laceration above the left eyebrow is a separate, superficial injury and is non-contributory.<br>2027: I can't tell from the record which injury came first or how much time separated them. He lived for some hours after he reached the hospital, so the bruising doesn't settle the order.</prop>`,
        `Ana tapped the lower one with the eraser end of her pencil and asked what it did with the first one's word.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`It says the 2007 doctor was wrong to call them separate.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Ana asks where it says that. It doesn't. Solberg never touches the word except to use it, separated, in her own sentence about time. A doctor who thought the first one got it wrong would have said so in her second paragraph, where she agrees with him about everything else.`,
          why:`Comparative passages. Contradicted. The review keeps the two injuries apart and never disputes the 2007 finding that they were separate.`},
        {t:`It agrees with the word, and says nothing more about it.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Ana says that's half of it. She agrees, all right. But she doesn't leave it there. Separate tells you there were two. It never says which one came first, and Solberg's whole paragraph is about exactly that, and about why she can't answer it.`,
          why:`Comparative passages. Half right. Solberg accepts that the injuries were separate, but she goes on to the question the word leaves open, the order and the time between them, and says the record can't settle it.`},
        {t:`It says the two injuries came close together in time.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Ana reads it out loud to her, slowly. Which came first or how much time separated them. She can't tell. That's not close together. That's nobody knows, said by somebody who'd know if anybody did.`,
          why:`Comparative passages. Contradicted. Solberg says she can't tell how much time separated the injuries.`},
        {t:`It takes up what separate left open, which came first.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:4},
          after:`Ana writes it in the margin, took up and couldn't close. Two injuries, the old report says, and stops. Solberg asks the next question, which one first and how long between, and then says the body can't tell her. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"And your brother's going to sit there and hear a doctor say she doesn't know which came first,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"He does. He's the only one in that room who does."</span>`,
          why:`Comparative passages. The 2007 report says only that the injuries were separate. Solberg's review takes up what that word leaves open, the order and the time between them, and says the record can't settle it. The second document asks the first one's unasked question and marks where the evidence stops.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s6`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR08`, title:`Her move`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday on the bus she read the reading Ana had sent her home with. Students in Seattle in 1974 who heard smashed instead of hit and remembered broken glass that was never in the film. Two psychologists in 2017 who said the first test of a memory, done right, tells you more than the courts thought. It wasn't so she'd know what to say, Ana had told her. It was so she'd know what Meredith was doing when she asked. They'd brought her father down to the county jail in Brighton that same morning.`,
        `He'd called the three Wednesdays since from Sterling and left her knight where it was. On the first she told him Lupe knew about him now, and that Lupe had asked if he could come to her party. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Tell her next one,"</span> he said, and then he talked about the heat.`,
        `Sunday, September 12, at seven, her phone rang from a number she didn't know, and the recording named the county jail instead of Sterling. She took it on the step anyway. It was where she took his calls.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"They bus you down in a cage with a window the size of a paperback,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I saw some corn. I saw Fort Morgan go by. Then I saw a wall."</span>`,
        `She told him first, before the board. Teo was getting up there tomorrow. Whatever her father said, to her, to Ana, to anybody.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I said no, mija. Twice. I told Brennan."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Teo told Ana you don't get a vote, Pa. You don't."</span>`,
        `Nothing on the line. A TV somewhere behind him, a different one from the tier's, and a man asking somebody for a pencil. She'd been working up to the rest of it since the carport. It was the only move she had.`,
        ((((all||{})[10]||{}).picks||{})[7]===1
          ?`TEO'S WORD, she'd written on the card in July. The pump at 11:05, the bridge, Abuela's porch. Nobody alive had seen any of it but Teo and the man on this phone.`
          :`The pump at 11:05, the bridge, Abuela's porch, all of it on one witness. Nobody alive had seen any of it but Teo and the man on this phone.`)],
      opts:[
        {t:`"She'll say he was ten, Pa. Nobody reads a clock right that late at ten."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"He read it right,"</span> her father says. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"He looked at it like it was a test."</span> Maybe Meredith will try the clock. It's a real hole, and it's the small one. The big one is who's doing the telling, and she went right past it.`,
          why:`Weaken. A real weakener, and not the strongest. Doubt about the clock touches one detail. The bigger problem is that the whole account comes from one witness with every reason to want it believed.`},
        {t:`"She'll say his DNA isn't under those nails, Pa. He was never in the lot."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"He never put a hand on him,"</span> her father says, which is right. A boy who swung a flashlight and never grabbed hold of anybody wouldn't leave a thing under a man's nails. Meredith knows that better than either of them. She'd never use it.`,
          why:`Weaken. It misreads the exclusion. Teo never had hold of Travis, so his DNA wouldn't be under the nails either way. It doesn't touch his account.`},
        {t:`"She'll say Ronnie never saw a boy in that bar, Pa. Not once all night."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Ronnie was pouring four deep,"</span> her father says. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Ronnie never looked down in his life."</span> Ronnie said as much in April, on the phone. A man not seeing a boy isn't the same as there being no boy.`,
          why:`Weaken. Weak. A bartender who never looked down not seeing a ten year old says very little about whether one was there.`},
        {t:`"She'll say he's your son, Pa, and nobody else saw that lot. Just you."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:2,health:4},
          after:`He's quiet long enough that she checks the screen. Then, low: <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"A son would say anything."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"That's what she'll say, Pa."</span> Not Ronnie, who never looked down. Not Consuelo, who heard a boy and saw her own kitchen in the glass.`,
          why:`Weaken. Teo's account rests on Teo alone, and Teo is Luis's son. The strongest attack gives his testimony a reason to exist whether or not it's true: a son wants his father home. It doesn't prove he's lying. It's a hole only another witness can close.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s7`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR19`, title:`Where the fight is`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`She said the rest of it before the minute warning, because there wasn't going to be another call. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"He can tell them he swung, Pa. He can't tell them why. He was ten. He was looking at the man."</span> The board was right in front of her and she didn't look at it. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Only one person can tell a judge why. It isn't Teo, and it isn't me."</span>`,
        `With the warning going he gave her his move. Knight out, the square, letter and number, the way he'd taught her. Then the line went dead. At the kitchen table she found the square and set his knight on it, facing hers across the middle of the board. If she wanted to trade, she could. He'd answered it. He hadn't said what else he'd answered.`,
        `At half past eight she drove to Federal, because Ana had said to come by if he called, and the light was on upstairs. Ana had two cards out on the desk, one for each side.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Ana's two cards">MEREDITH: THE JURY KNEW HE FOUGHT HIM. THE DNA SAYS HE FOUGHT HIM. ANOTHER MAN IN A PACKED BAR ON A SERIES NIGHT TOUCHED HIM. THAT'S A CROWD BRUSHING PAST HIM, NOT A MAN WHO HAD HOLD OF HIM, AND NOT AN ATTACKER.<br>ANA: THE STATE NEEDED ONE MAN TO HAVE HAD HOLD OF HIM THAT NIGHT. THERE WERE TWO. A JURY THAT HEARD THAT PROBABLY DOESN'T CONVICT ON A JACKET.</prop>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Ten minutes each in the morning,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Where's the fight? Not the case. The fight on these two cards."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Whether Pa fought Travis Hensley in the bar that Saturday night.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Read mine again,"</span> Ana says. Nothing on her card denies the fight. Nobody's going to deny the fight tomorrow, least of all her father, if he does what she thinks he's going to do.`,
          why:`Point at issue. Both cards accept that Luis fought Travis. A claim both sides accept can't be the point they dispute.`},
        {t:`Whether the second profile means another man had hold of him.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's the whole hearing,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"She says it's a crowd. I say a man had hold of him. Same two pages from the lab, read two ways."</span> She draws a line from one card to the other.`,
          why:`Point at issue. Meredith says the second profile is ordinary contact in a crowd. Ana says it shows a second man had hold of him. One says yes and the other says no, about the same thing.`},
        {t:`Whether the DNA shows who hit Travis Hensley at the curb.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"I never say who,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Find who on my card."</span> She can't. Meredith says it wasn't an attacker. Ana never says it was. That's one speaker on it, not two.`,
          why:`Point at issue. Close. Meredith denies the second man attacked him, but Ana never claims he did. A point at issue needs both speakers to take a side.`},
        {t:`Whether the 2008 jury got it wrong about the jacket.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Ana says Meredith won't touch 2008 tomorrow and neither will she. Neither card says one word about that jury. What they're fighting over is two pages from a lab, dated last month.`,
          why:`Point at issue. Neither card addresses whether the 2008 jury erred. That's the case in the larger sense, not what these two statements dispute.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s8`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR20`, title:`Line two`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At eleven, with her bag packed, Ana opened the top drawer and took the opinion out and laid it open on the desk to page eleven. The soft copy, the coffee ring. The small circled 1 in the margin beside the one sentence.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"I put that there the week after,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"The night after a girl sat across from me and asked what they had to believe. Not before I filed. After. The brief had it ninth."</span> She looked at the chair Dani was sitting in, the one across the desk. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That chair was in my office at the division. I brought it with me when I left. You sat in it with your mother's car keys in your lap and asked me what they had to believe. I knew. I wrote eight other things."</span>`,
        `Dani had the keys before she had anything else. Ma's ring, with the grocery store tags and the brass tab from the hospital lot. She'd held them so hard she had the marks after, all the way home in Ma's car. She'd kept the no all these years. She hadn't kept the face.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Dani">"I didn't know it was you,"</span> Dani said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"I know,"</span> Ana said. She was looking at the chair, not at her. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"You came up those stairs in November with that transcript and sat down in it like it was any chair."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"You never answered me."</span> Ana put the pencil down. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"I've been answering you since that night."</span> Then she closed the opinion and put it back in the drawer, face up this time, and told Dani to go home and sleep, and that tomorrow she'd sit behind the rail and help nobody.`,
        `At home she hung her keys on the hook under Ma's lanyard and stood in the dark kitchen a while before she went up.`,
        `Monday, September 13, the lot at the Justice Center in Brighton was already full at a quarter past eight, and the line at the metal detector went out the door, and the man in front of her had to walk back to his car with a pocketknife. By the elevators Meredith Sloane stopped Ana with a coffee in each hand and gave her one, and Dani was two steps behind and heard all of it. <span class="said" data-who="Meredith">"He walked up your stairs and gave my investigator the inside of his cheek before anybody knew a thing. I've wondered since July. Why?"</span> Ana told her to ask him. Meredith said she wouldn't be asking him that on the stand, and pushed three.`,
        ((s.flags.wroteshannon||G.wroteshannon)
          ?`Upstairs, outside the courtroom doors, Meredith took Ana aside for a minute, and Ana came back with her coffee and said, low, that Meredith said Shannon had the letter. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"She read every word of it, Dani. She's in the second row with it in her purse."</span> Ana didn't say anything else about it, then or after, and when they went in, there was Shannon, by herself, with the purse on her knees.`
          :`Shannon Hensley was already in the second row on the State's side when they went in, by herself, with her purse on her knees and both hands on the clasp, looking at the seal on the wall over the bench like she was reading it.`),
        `A deputy brought her father in the side door and sat him down next to Ana, and he turned around in his chair and found Teo in the front row next to Celeste Abeyta and her legal pad. The last time the two of them were in one room was Ma's viewing, eleven years back. Teo looked back at him till the deputy said something and Pa turned around to the front.`,
        `They each did their ten minutes, more or less what was on Ana's cards, and then Meredith stayed standing and said she had one more thing, in case anybody meant to call the son's account new, or the boy in the lot. <span class="said" data-who="Meredith">"If the son was in that lot, his father brought him there and drove him away and has known it every day since. Whatever the son says, it isn't new."</span>`,
        `Ana got up with the March card in her hand, her thumb on line two, and said the People were right about that, and Judge Esquibel wrote something on his pad. <span class="said" data-who="Esquibel">"Noted. Then what's new is the report."</span> Ana said yes, Judge, the report, and sat back down.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`That the boy in the lot was Teo, and that he was there all night.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Meredith never said it was Teo. She said if. Ana hasn't said it in court either, not yet. Teo's still in the front row with his hands between his knees, waiting to be the one who says it.`,
          why:`Point of agreement. Meredith's point is conditional: if the son was there, his father knew. She doesn't concede the boy was Teo, so neither of them has committed to it.`},
        {t:`That whatever Teo says isn't new. If he was there, Pa knew.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Meredith sits too, with the face of somebody who brought an umbrella and found the sun out. Line two was settled in about six minutes by the clock over the door, which ran a minute fast, and the rest of the day was going to be the left hand and the people who could tell a judge what it meant.`,
          why:`Point of agreement. Meredith says that if the son was in the lot, his father has known it all along, so whatever the son says isn't new. Ana says the People are right about that. A concession is a point of agreement in the speaker's own words.`},
        {t:`That the DNA report is the only thing the judge should hear.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Esquibel said the report is what's new. That isn't the same as only. Ana has three witnesses on her list for today alone, and Meredith hasn't objected to one of them.`,
          why:`Point of agreement. Close, and still not it. Esquibel says the report is what's new and Ana agrees, but Meredith never says so, and nobody says nothing else may be heard. Ana is still calling Teo, and Meredith doesn't object.`},
        {t:`That Teo's account alone would probably get Pa an acquittal.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Meredith would sooner sit down for good than say that. Ana hasn't said it either. She's careful never to say probably about anything but the jacket, and she isn't going to start now.`,
          why:`Point of agreement. Ana might argue something like it, and Meredith rejects it. At most that's a point at issue, never a point of agreement.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s9`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR07`, title:`July sixteenth`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana called Teo first. He went up past the rail in his good shirt, the blue one from Ma's viewing, and Celeste Abeyta slid down to the end of the front row so he'd be able to see her, and he put his hand up for the oath a little too high, like a kid in school.`,
        `Twenty five minutes. Twice he got the order wrong, the Sprites and then the tailgate, and went back and fixed it himself without anybody asking him to. Ana let him. The court reporter asked him once to slow down. In the lot part he said Papi. He just said it, in front of the judge and the deputy and Travis Hensley's sister, and went on to the truck.`,
        ((s.picks||{})[3]===2
          ?`Dani looked at her shoes for most of it, the brown ones with the scuffed toe, like she'd told him she would.`
          :`Dani looked at her shoes for most of it, the brown ones with the scuffed toe. She couldn't watch him do it and not help.`),
        `Meredith didn't raise her voice once. Was he ten that night. Yes, ma'am. Had he believed for twenty years that he'd killed Travis Hensley. Yes. Had his father been in prison most of that time. Yes, and he looked at his hands on the rail when he said it. <span class="said" data-who="Meredith">"Nobody saw your father at that gas station but you. Nobody saw that bridge but you. Nobody has ever found a flashlight."</span> He said no, ma'am. Last she asked if he loved his father. Teo looked over at their father's table, the first time he'd looked there since he put his hand up. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Yes, ma'am."</span> Meredith said she had nothing further.`,
        `Esquibel gave them ten minutes before redirect. Ana came to the rail and bent down so only Dani would hear. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"I get one question,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Which?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"You're under oath, Mr. Ruiz. Is everything you told this court true?"`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Ana asks it. Teo says yes, all of it. Of course he does, and a son lying for his father would say the same thing under the same oath, and Meredith doesn't bother to object. It costs nothing to ask and proves nothing, and what Meredith built is still standing when he steps down.`,
          why:`Strengthen. An oath doesn't answer a claim of bias. A biased witness gives the same answer under the same oath, so it adds nothing.`},
        {t:`"Mr. Ruiz, whose DNA did the lab find under the man's right hand?"`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Ana asks it. Teo says he isn't a scientist, and Meredith objects, and Esquibel sustains it. The report's already in. It proves their father fought the man, which nobody denies, and it does nothing about whether his son would lie for him.`,
          why:`Strengthen. Luis's profile under the right hand fits Teo's account of the fight, but the fight is conceded. It doesn't answer the bias attack, and Teo isn't the witness to read a lab report.`},
        {t:`"When you gave your sample on July sixteenth, what did you think it would show?"`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:3,health:4},
          after:`Ana asks it. Teo looks at Celeste, and then at nobody. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"That it was me,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Under there. I thought it was going to say it was me."</span> Ana asks who sealed it. A man from the district attorney's office, he says, at Ana's desk, and they both signed. Meredith writes something down. She'd asked why in the hall that morning. Now it's in the record, in his voice.`,
          why:`Strengthen. Meredith's attack is that a son would say anything to free his father. A son who hands the State his own DNA while he still believes it will show he killed a man isn't shaping evidence for anybody. It was fixed in place, by the State's own investigator, before anyone knew what it would show.`},
        {t:`"You stayed away from your father ten years. Why would you lie for him now?"`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Ana asks it, and Teo says he couldn't sit in a room with him. It cuts the wrong way. Ten years away were ten years of keeping the story their father gave him at the casket, and Meredith only has to stand up and say so.`,
          why:`Strengthen. Distance from his father doesn't show independence from his father's story. Staying away was part of keeping it, so the bias attack stands.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s10`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC14`, title:`A curb`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`After lunch Ana called Dr. Solberg, who wore a gray suit and reading glasses on a cord and answered every question as if somebody she respected had asked it. For forty minutes she said what her letter said, in nearly the same words. The fracture at the back of the skull killed him. Its features fit a blow from an object or the back of his head striking a hard edge, like a curb, and the findings alone couldn't tell her which. The cut above the eye was real and shallow and played no part in his death. She couldn't say which injury came first or how long came between them. When Ana asked whether the cut could have put him on the ground, she said nothing in the record showed that it did.`,
        `Meredith's cross was short. She read the opinion paragraph of the 2007 report out loud, slowly, to its last words. <span class="said" data-who="Meredith">"The manner of death is homicide. Doctor, your letter says a curb. Are you telling this court Mr. Hensley fell?"</span> Solberg said she wasn't telling the court anything the findings didn't. A blow or a curb, and she couldn't choose, and wouldn't. <span class="said" data-who="Meredith">"And a steel flashlight could have made that fracture."</span> An object could have, Solberg said. She'd never seen the object. <span class="said" data-who="Meredith">"Nobody has,"</span> Meredith said, and sat down.`,
        `In the front row Teo had gone still, the way he went still under a bus when he heard something he didn't like. Dani knew what he'd just heard. She also knew where his swing had landed. Above the eye. She'd told him so at his own kitchen table.`,
        `Driving home down 85 at six, Dani held the two pages in her head side by side, the way Ana had laid them on the desk. Homicide on one. A curb on the other. For about a second in that courtroom she'd thought the two of them couldn't both be right.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Solberg agrees he died of the fracture, and disagrees with 2007 over how it happened.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Disagrees is the word that's wrong. The 2007 report never said how, beyond blunt force. Solberg can't disagree with a sentence nobody wrote, and she spent forty minutes not doing it.`,
          why:`Comparative passages. Half right. They agree on the fatal injury, but the 2007 report never said how it happened beyond blunt force, so there's nothing there for Solberg to disagree with.`},
        {t:`Solberg's curb takes back the word homicide that 2007 wrote down.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Solberg said no to that, out loud, under oath. Homicide is about what somebody did, and a man knocked over backward onto a curb has had something done to him.`,
          why:`Comparative passages. Contradicted. Solberg accepted the manner of death. A curb doesn't rule out homicide.`},
        {t:`2007 says a curb, and Solberg adds that it could have been an object.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`She has them backward. The old report never once says curb. The curb is Solberg's word, with an object right next to it, and Meredith was reading from the other page.`,
          why:`Comparative passages. Reversed. The 2007 report mentions no curb. The curb comes from Solberg's review.`},
        {t:`They fit. Blunt force covers a blow or a fall. 2007 never named what hit him.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:4},
          after:`Blunt force injury of the head, the old report says. Not an object. Not a curb. Anything hard that meets a head fast. Solberg didn't take back a word of it. She said blunt force could have been a thing in somebody's hand or the edge of a curb, and that was as far as fourteen photographs would take her. A man knocked over backward onto a curb by somebody's swing is still a homicide. Meredith had read the two pages like they were arguing, and they weren't.`,
          why:`Comparative passages. The 2007 report gives a cause, blunt force injury, and a manner, homicide, without naming the instrument. Solberg keeps both and says the findings fit an object or a curb. The review spells out what the first report left open. It doesn't contradict it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s11`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR02`, title:`What it's doing`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday Alma walked her grandmother in on her arm and sat down right behind her, and Consuelo Baca took the oath in a navy dress with a brooch at the collar and said I do like she'd been waiting since 2007 for somebody to ask.`,
        `Ana asked her what she saw. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"A man in a red jacket and a ball cap hit that man with something, and he went over backward on the curb, and the one in the cap ran."</span> Ana asked about the face. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"I never saw a face. I said so then."</span> Ana asked whether she'd heard anything earlier. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"A little boy crying for his papi. Ten to eleven, by my stove. I told the young officer. I told the detective. Nobody asked me again until her."</span> She nodded at Dani, behind the rail.`,
        ((s.flags.ledconsuelo||G.ledconsuelo)
          ?`Meredith had a page of Dani's notes from February. <span class="said" data-who="Meredith">"Mrs. Baca, a young woman asked you in your kitchen whether the man was about her father's height, and you said maybe five ten."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"She asked me. I said maybe, to be nice. Then I told her what I'm telling you. Smaller than the big one. That's what I saw, señora."</span> Dani had written that question down herself.`
          :`Meredith stood up for one question. <span class="said" data-who="Meredith">"Mrs. Baca, you can't tell this court the man you saw wasn't Luis Ruiz."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"I can't tell you who he was. I never could. I told them that in 2007, and they wrote down the jacket."</span>`),
        ((s.flags.calledgenealiar||G.calledgenealiar)
          ?`Gene Vigil came up after her in a sport coat and didn't look at Dani once, coming or going. He testified the way a man reads his own report: the crew list, twenty one names, nobody on it interviewed but Luis Ruiz; the cap, written and struck; the scrapings, cancelled in November on his say. When Ana asked about the margin beside Consuelo's statement, he answered her looking at the clock. <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"I wrote the word boy in that margin at nine in the morning, with a question mark. At three that afternoon I said excuse me, son, to a boy on the Ruiz basement stairs."</span>`
          :`Gene Vigil came up after her in a sport coat and nodded to Dani on his way past the rail. He testified the way a man reads his own report: the crew list, twenty one names, nobody on it interviewed but Luis Ruiz; the cap, written and struck; the scrapings, cancelled in November on his say. When Ana asked about the margin beside Consuelo's statement, he answered her looking at Teo. <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"I wrote the word boy in that margin at nine in the morning, with a question mark. At three that afternoon I said excuse me, son, to a boy on the Ruiz basement stairs."</span>`),
        `At eleven the State called Dani. She went up past her father without looking at him, which was the only way she could do it. Meredith asked her what she'd seen in the washer on the morning of October 28, 2007. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"My father's red work jacket. It was wet."</span> Had she seen her father that night? <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"No. I was at a sleepover in Thornton."</span> Had the machine run? <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"The cycle was done. It was wet. That's what I saw."</span> Ana had no questions. Coming back down she looked at him, because now she could. Eleven minutes, start to finish, and nothing in it she hadn't said at fifteen, because it was all she'd ever seen.`,
        `At lunch, at a table in the cafeteria downstairs, Ana wrote her closing on one card and pushed it across.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Ana's closing, on one card">IN 2008 THE STATE SAID HE LIED ABOUT THE FIGHT, AND A MAN WHO LIES ABOUT A FIGHT IS HIDING A KILLING.<br>HIS SON HAS TOLD THIS COURT HE HIT TRAVIS HENSLEY IN THAT LOT, AND HIS FATHER DROVE HIM AWAY.<br>A FATHER WITH A TEN YEAR OLD WHO'D DONE THAT HAS A REASON TO LIE THAT ISN'T THE CURB.<br>SO THE LIE DOESN'T POINT AT THE CURB.</prop>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Line two,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Your brother hit him. What's it doing on my card?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`It's the conclusion. Teo hit him, and Pa didn't.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Find who hit him at the curb on my card,"</span> Ana says. It isn't there. The last line is about the lie, not about who. Teo's swing is something the card stands on, not the place it ends up.`,
          why:`Role. The conclusion is the last line: the lie doesn't point at the curb. Teo's admission is a premise, and the card never says who struck the fatal blow.`},
        {t:`It shows a boy was in the lot. That's all it's for.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"It does that,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Consuelo did that for me at ten this morning. That's not why it's on the card."</span> The line ties the boy to her father's reason for lying, and the card needs that and nothing less.`,
          why:`Role. True of the statement, and not its role here. On this card the admission isn't offered to place a boy in the lot. It's offered to explain the lie.`},
        {t:`It gives Pa's lie a reason that isn't the curb.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's its whole job,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"The State built on the lie. I'm not going to say he didn't lie. I'm going to hand them the reason."</span> She underlines HIS SON once. The lie stays a lie. It just stops being evidence of the one thing the State used it for.`,
          why:`Role. The State used Luis's lie as a sign that he killed Travis. Teo's admission is a premise offering another explanation for the lie, a father protecting a son who'd hit a man. With that in place, the lie no longer supports the State's inference, which is the card's conclusion.`},
        {t:`It concedes the State's point, and the card answers it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"What point?"</span> Ana says. The State never said there was a boy, let alone that he swung. You can't concede a point nobody made, and the State has never made it.`,
          why:`Role. A concession grants something the other side argued. The State never argued Teo was there. The admission is Ana's own premise.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s12`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR15`, title:`Anything`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At two Ana stood up and said, <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"The defense calls Luis Ruiz."</span> Nobody had told Dani. She found out with the rest of the room. A deputy walked him from the table to the stand, and he took the oath with his hand flat, and sat, and laid both hands on the rail of the box where everybody could see them. She had the envelope in her bag. She didn't take it out.`,
        `Ana kept it short, and mostly she let him talk. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I hit him first. In the bar. He had my boy by the arm, shaking him, and my boy's ten, he's been after me since Thursday about that game, and I hit the man before I thought about it. Ronnie put me out the back. The man came out after me and had me bent back over the tailgate of my truck and I couldn't get up. My son got the flashlight from behind the seat and hit him one time to get him off me."</span> He looked at the judge. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"He was ten years old and that man had his father on a tailgate. That's why he swung. Nobody else can tell you that. I was the one under him."</span> The man let go and walked off holding his face, he said, and he took his boy to his mother's in Globeville, and he never saw that man again.`,
        `She'd told him that on a jail phone Sunday night. He'd carried it up the three steps and put it down in front of a judge like it was his. In the second row Shannon Hensley's hands hadn't moved off the clasp of her purse.`,
        `Meredith let a few seconds go by before she stood up, and when she did she left her notes on the table. Had he told Detective Vigil he never touched anybody that night? Yes, ma'am, he had, and it was a lie. Had he told Vigil he drove home alone at a quarter to eleven? <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"That was a lie too."</span> Then why should this court believe a man who'd told the same two lies since 2007. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I told them so nobody would come looking for my boy,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"He's sitting right there. There's nobody left to look for."</span>`,
        `He said it to Meredith and not to the room. Meredith wrote one word on her pad and said she had nothing further for this witness.`,
        `Ana's closing took twelve minutes, the card from lunch nearly word for word and then the left hand, with her reading glasses pushed up on her head the whole time. Meredith went second.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Meredith">"Nothing here proves anything, and Ms. Brennan has been careful never to say it does,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Meredith">"What we have is a second man who touched Travis Hensley at some point, in a bar with a hundred and twenty people in it on a World Series night. And we have a man who's believed for twenty years that he killed somebody, telling you a story nobody else in the world saw. Teo Ruiz loves his father. I believe that. A son would say anything to bring his father home, Judge, and we can't rely on a word of it."</span>`,
        ((s.flags.overclaimed||G.overclaimed)
          ?`Proves. She'd typed it into the draft in March at the kitchen table, at one in the morning, and Ana had circled it twice in pencil, and here it was coming out of Meredith's mouth about a word Ana had never once used in that courtroom.`
          :`A son would say anything. Meredith said it like it closed the subject, and Dani sat behind the rail with her bag on her knees trying to work out, in plain words, the way she would have on a Tuesday night with the practice book, what kind of argument that actually was.`)],
      opts:[
        {t:`The coach wants his team to win. His prediction that they'll win is probably hopeful.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Same kind of man, somebody who wants something. But probably hopeful is about what you'd say about any coach in August, and it's fair. It takes the motive into account and keeps the man. Meredith had thrown out every word Teo said.`,
          why:`Parallel flaw. Close in content, not in form. Treating a motive as a reason for some caution is reasonable. Meredith's argument goes further and discards everything the witness said.`},
        {t:`The witness stood a block off at night. Her account of the car's color is hard to rely on.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`She'd have said the same thing about Consuelo, at her window across the street at night, and so would Ana. You'd wonder about a color from there. A reason to check, sure. Meredith hadn't checked anything. Meredith never said Teo was too far away to see. She said he was his father's son.`,
          why:`Parallel flaw. It discounts a witness for a reason about what she could see, not her motive, and its conclusion is measured. There's no matching flaw.`},
        {t:`The dairy board paid the author, and a paid author writes anything. Throw out the study.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`That's the one. The dairy board paid for it, so nobody has to read it, and you can throw it in the trash without opening it. A paid study can still be right. You'd have to open it to find out. Meredith had walked right past the cheek swab on July sixteenth to get to where she wanted to go.`,
          why:`Parallel flaw. Meredith goes from the witness's motive to rejecting everything he said, without weighing it against evidence like the sample he gave before he knew. The study argument makes the same move: the author has a reason to say it, so none of it can be believed.`},
        {t:`Every honest witness keeps his story straight. This one has. He's an honest witness.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Wrong mistake. It runs a rule the wrong way, honest to steady and back, and never once asks who's doing the talking. Meredith's argument was nothing but who.`,
          why:`Parallel flaw. A different flaw, reversing a conditional. Meredith's argument dismisses testimony because of the speaker's motive.`}
      ]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f11s12`, title:`Who said it, and what it proves`, intro:`Ana's last cards of the summer, written Tuesday night from her own notes of the hearing. One line on each card, and who said it. Pick what the line proves, and nothing more.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Card one. Consuelo, on direct: "I never saw a face. I said so then."`, choices:[`The man at the curb wasn't Luis.`,`Her eyesight was poor that night.`,`She can't identify the man by his face.`], answer:2, why:`She's saying what she didn't see. Without a face she can't identify anybody by it, so it rules nobody in and nobody out. It doesn't make her a weak witness, and it doesn't clear Luis either.`},
      {prompt:`Card two. Gene, on direct: "I wrote the word boy in that margin at nine in the morning, with a question mark. At three that afternoon I said excuse me, son, to a boy on the Ruiz basement stairs."`, choices:[`He noted a possible boy in the morning and passed a boy in the afternoon.`,`He knew by Sunday night that the boy in the lot was Teo.`,`Teo was the boy Consuelo heard crying.`], answer:0, why:`Two facts from one day, set side by side. The line doesn't say he connected them, and it can't prove whose crying Consuelo heard.`},
      {prompt:`Card three. Dani, for the State: "The cycle was done. It was wet. That's what I saw."`, choices:[`Her father washed the jacket to get blood out.`,`The jacket was wet, and the cycle had finished, when she saw it.`,`Her father ran the washer the night before.`], answer:1, why:`What she saw and only that: a finished cycle and a wet jacket. Nothing in the line says who ran it, when, or why.`},
      {prompt:`Card four. Luis, on cross, about driving home alone at a quarter to eleven: "That was a lie too."`, choices:[`Everything he told the police was false.`,`He was at the curb at a quarter past eleven.`,`He didn't drive home alone at a quarter to eleven.`], answer:2, why:`He's admitting one statement was false. That doesn't make every statement false, and it doesn't put him anywhere at a quarter past eleven.`},
      {prompt:`Card five. Meredith, after Solberg says she never saw the object: "Nobody has."`, choices:[`The flashlight was never recovered.`,`There was never any flashlight.`,`Teo made up the flashlight.`], answer:0, why:`Never found isn't never existed. The line is about the evidence room, not about the lot.`},
      {prompt:`Card six. Solberg, in Ana's notes: a blow or a curb, and she couldn't choose.`, choices:[`He fell and hit the curb.`,`Someone struck the back of his head.`,`The findings fit either one.`], answer:2, why:`She declined to choose, so either single cause goes past her. Both stay open.`}
    ]},
  passages: {
    p1: { title:`Colorado Bureau of Investigation, forensic DNA report, summary of results, August 2027`,
      text:[`Item 7. Fingernail scrapings and clippings, right and left hands of Travis Hensley, collected at autopsy, October 28, 2007.`,
        `Reference samples compared: Travis Hensley (blood standard, 2007). Luis Ruiz (2027). Teo Ruiz (collected July 16, 2027).`,
        `Right hand. A mixture of DNA from two people. Travis Hensley is included as a contributor. Luis Ruiz is included as a contributor. Teo Ruiz is excluded.`,
        `Left hand. A mixture of DNA from two people. Travis Hensley is included as a contributor. The other contributor is male and is designated Unknown Male 1. Luis Ruiz and Teo Ruiz are excluded as the source of Unknown Male 1.`,
        `The profile of Unknown Male 1 was searched in the state and national DNA databases. No match was found.`,
        `These results identify whose DNA is present. They do not establish when, or by what kind of contact, any of it was deposited.`] },
    p2: { title:`Ana's reading for the weekend: two passages on what a witness remembers`,
      text:[`Passage A`,
        `In 1974 the psychologists Elizabeth Loftus and John Palmer, at the University of Washington, showed students short films of traffic accidents and then asked how fast the cars had been going. Everyone got the same question except for one word. Some were asked how fast the cars were going when they hit each other. Others heard smashed, collided, bumped or contacted. The word moved the answers. Students who heard smashed put the speed at about 41 miles an hour on average, and students who heard contacted put it at about 32, though they had watched the same films. Four of the films showed crashes staged at known speeds, and the true speed did far less to sway the estimates than the wording did.`,
        `A second experiment asked whether the word had changed the memory or only the answer. One hundred fifty students watched a film in which the accident lasted four seconds. Fifty were asked the smashed question, fifty the hit question, and fifty weren't asked about speed at all. A week later, without seeing the film again, they were asked whether they had seen any broken glass. There was none in the film. Sixteen of the fifty who had heard smashed said yes, against seven of those who had heard hit and six of those asked nothing. The authors proposed that a memory of an event takes in what was seen and also what is supplied afterward, and that over time the two merge, until the witness can't say which detail came from where.`,
        `Passage B`,
        `For years the lesson drawn from studies like that one was that memory bends too easily to be trusted, and American courts came more and more to treat an eyewitness's confidence as telling them little. In 2017 the psychologists John Wixted and Gary Wells argued that the lesson had been drawn too broadly. They didn't deny that memory can be contaminated. Their point was that the doubt had grown up around poor police procedures, and that a memory's first test, given properly, tells you more than the courts had come to believe.`,
        `They described what they called pristine conditions. The test comes first, before the memory has been worked over. The lineup is fair, so the suspect doesn't stand out. Nobody running it can steer the witness. The witness's confidence is taken down at once. Reviewing laboratory studies of staged crimes and studies from police departments, they found that for adults under those conditions, confidence and accuracy go together, and an identification made with high confidence is very likely to be right. A hesitant first identification is a warning, and they argued that taking it seriously would help clear innocent people.`,
        `Their claims are about that first test. When the conditions aren't met, as when a lineup is unfair, even a confident identification is seriously compromised, and their argument doesn't reach a witness who has been questioned and shown photographs for months before trial.`] }
  },
  quiz: [
    {q:`Archaeologist: Bronze pins found at the Kell site match pins from the coastal settlement of Varo in both their alloy and their shape. Pins like these were costly to make and were seldom carried far from where they were made. So the people of Kell must have traded directly with Varo.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most seriously weakens the archaeologist's argument?`,
      opts:[`Pins of the same alloy and shape were also made at several inland sites, two nearer to Kell than Varo.`,`The people of Varo made many kinds of bronze objects besides pins, including tools and small cast figures.`,`Some of the pins found at the Kell site show signs of long use and of careful repair.`,`Archaeologists still disagree about when the Kell site was first settled and by whom.`,`Bronze pins have turned up at a number of other sites along the coast near Varo.`],
      a:0, why:`Weaken. The argument needs the pins to point to Varo and nowhere else. If identical pins were made at inland sites nearer to Kell, the match no longer singles out Varo. Varo's other crafts, the pins' wear, the dating dispute and other coastal finds don't touch that link.`, task:`LR08`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Company report: Last year 300 of our employees joined a voluntary lunchtime walking program. Compared with the year before, the employees who walked took about a quarter fewer sick days. The program clearly reduces sick days, and we should expand it.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most seriously weakens the argument in the report?`,
      opts:[`Some employees who joined the program stopped walking regularly after a few months.`,`The walking route runs through a city park a short way from the company's main office.`,`Sick days also fell by about a quarter last year among employees who didn't join.`,`Employees who walk at lunch say they feel more alert during their afternoon meetings.`,`The company also offers a voluntary class on healthy cooking that meets once a week.`],
      a:2, why:`Weaken. If sick days fell just as much among people who didn't join, the drop has a cause that has nothing to do with the program. Some walkers quitting would, if anything, make the result more striking. The route, the feeling of alertness and the cooking class don't explain the fall.`, task:`LR08`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`CAUSE`,`STAT`]},
    {q:`Naturalist: The swifts that nest in the old mill chimney left two weeks earlier this autumn than in any year on record. The flying insects they feed on dropped sharply in number over August. The early departure was most likely caused by the shortage of food.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the naturalist's argument?`,
      opts:[`Swifts are among the fastest flying birds that nest anywhere in the whole region.`,`Swifts forty miles off, where insect numbers held steady through August, left on time.`,`The old mill chimney where the swifts nest is scheduled to be capped next spring.`,`In several earlier years, insect numbers in August were also well below average.`,`Many other species of birds also leave the region every autumn for warmer places south.`],
      a:1, why:`Strengthen. Swifts that kept their food supply and left on schedule act like a comparison group: the birds with the shortage left early, the birds without it didn't. Speed and the chimney's future are irrelevant, earlier years with low insect numbers and no departure this early (the stimulus says it's the earliest on record) would weaken the argument, and other species leaving says nothing about timing.`, task:`LR07`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`CAUSE`]},
    {q:`Historian: In a diary entry from 1889, Ada Quill wrote that she had accidentally started the fire that destroyed her family's mill, a fire blamed at the time on a worker who was dismissed. Some scholars think the entry is a later forgery meant to clear the worker. But the entry is probably genuine.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the historian's claim?`,
      opts:[`The worker blamed for the fire had a large family and never found other work in the town.`,`Ada Quill kept a diary for more than forty years and wrote in it nearly every single day.`,`Several other mills in the region burned down during the 1880s, most of them by accident.`,`The entry matches the ink and hand around it, and the diary stayed sealed until 1960.`,`The scholars who doubt the entry have exposed several other forged documents in the past.`],
      a:3, why:`Strengthen. Matching ink and handwriting, and a diary sealed until 1960, make a later insertion much harder to believe. The worker's family explains a motive for forgery, if anything. A long diary habit, other mill fires and the doubters' record don't bear on whether this entry is real.`, task:`LR07`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Imani: Moving the farmers market to Thursday evenings has been a success. Three times as many people come as came on Saturday mornings.<br>Paul: Three times as many people come, but most of them leave without buying anything. For the farmers, a success would mean selling more produce, and they're selling less than they did on Saturdays.<br><br>Imani and Paul disagree over whether`,
      opts:[`more people come to the market on Thursday evenings than came on Saturday mornings`,`moving the market to Thursday evenings has been a success`,`the farmers would prefer to move the market back to Saturday mornings`,`most of the people who come on Thursday evenings leave without buying anything`,`the market should be held on both Thursdays and Saturdays`],
      a:1, why:`Point at issue. Imani says the move has been a success. Paul says a success would mean more sales, and sales are down, so it hasn't been. Paul grants the bigger crowds, Imani never addresses who buys, and neither speaks to the farmers' wishes or to holding the market twice.`, task:`LR19`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Okoro: The river trail should be lit at night. Joggers use it after dark, and two of them were hurt in falls there last winter.<br>Fenn: Lighting the whole trail would cost more than the parks budget allows. But both of last winter's falls happened under the rail bridge, where the trail is darkest, and that stretch at least should be lit.<br><br>Okoro and Fenn are committed to agreeing that`,
      opts:[`the parks budget should be raised to pay for lighting the trail`,`the entire river trail should be lit at night`,`at least part of the river trail should be lit at night`,`joggers should stay off the river trail after dark`,`the stretch under the rail bridge is the trail's busiest part`],
      a:2, why:`Point of agreement. Okoro wants the whole trail lit, which includes every part of it. Fenn wants the stretch under the bridge lit. Both are committed to lighting at least part of it. Fenn says the whole trail is beyond the budget, neither proposes raising the budget or closing the trail, and nobody says which stretch is busiest.`, task:`LR20`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Engineer: In the three months since repairs were finished on the Teller Street bridge, sensors on its supports have recorded no unusual stress. During those months the bridge has been open only to cars and light vans. So the repairs have made the bridge safe for the heavy trucks the city plans to allow back next month.<br><br>The reasoning in the engineer's argument is flawed because it`,
      opts:[`draws a conclusion about heavy trucks from months when no heavy trucks used the bridge`,`treats the city's plan to reopen the bridge to trucks as evidence about the bridge's condition`,`fails to say how many cars and vans crossed the bridge during the three months`,`takes for granted that the bridge was unsafe for trucks before the repairs were made`,`relies on a sample of bridges that is too small to support a general conclusion`],
      a:0, why:`Flaw. Three quiet months under light traffic say little about loads the bridge hasn't carried since the repairs. The argument never leans on the city's plan, doesn't need a traffic count, doesn't depend on how unsafe the bridge was before, and is about one bridge, not a sample.`, task:`LR06`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Editorial: Some say the county should widen Route 9 to shorten commutes. It's true that traffic on Route 9 has doubled in ten years. But in counties like ours, widened roads have filled with new traffic within a few years. So widening Route 9 would shorten commutes only briefly. The county should therefore spend the money on the commuter rail line instead.<br><br>The claim that widening Route 9 would shorten commutes only briefly plays which one of the following roles in the editorial's argument?`,
      opts:[`It is the main conclusion that the editorial as a whole argues for.`,`It is drawn from evidence and supports the editorial's main conclusion.`,`It is a concession to the view that the editorial goes on to oppose.`,`It is a claim the editorial attributes to others and then goes on to reject.`,`It is background that describes the problem the editorial addresses.`],
      a:1, why:`Role. It's supported by the evidence about other counties, and it supports the last claim, that the money should go to the rail line. That makes it an intermediate conclusion. The doubling of traffic is the concession, the rail line is the main conclusion, and the editorial endorses this claim rather than rejecting it.`, task:`LR02`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Principle: A museum should return an object to the community it came from if the object was taken without that community's consent and the community has asked for it back. A museum that keeps such an object after that request holds it on terms the community never accepted.<br><br>Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle above?`,
      opts:[`The Aldine Museum should return a set of masks a village sold to a collector in 1920, now that the village has asked for them.`,`The Brandt Gallery should return a statue taken from a temple without permission, though no one from the temple's town has asked.`,`The city museum should keep a pottery collection a farmer gave it in 1950, though the farmer's grandchildren want it back.`,`The Vossler Museum should return a door taken from the village of Orra over its elders' objections, which Orra has asked for.`,`The Corwin Museum should return a painting to a family that sold it during hard times, if the family can afford to buy it back.`],
      a:3, why:`Apply the principle. The door was taken without consent, and Orra has asked for it, so both conditions are met. The masks were sold, so consent was given. No one has asked for the statue. The principle says when to return an object, not when to keep one, so it can't support keeping the pottery. The painting was sold.`, task:`LR13`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`All of the tomatoes sold at the Sorrel farm stand are grown without pesticides. The tomatoes in this basket, which were bought on Saturday at the market downtown, were grown without pesticides. So the tomatoes in this basket came from the Sorrel farm stand.<br><br>Which one of the following arguments contains flawed reasoning most similar to that in the argument above?`,
      opts:[`Every member of the Linnet choir can read music. Iker is a member of the choir. So Iker can read music.`,`No member of the Linnet choir is paid to sing. Iker is paid to sing. So Iker isn't a member of the choir.`,`Most members of the Linnet choir can read music. Iker is a member of the choir. So Iker can probably read music.`,`Every member of the Linnet choir can read music. Iker isn't a member of the choir. So Iker can't read music.`,`Every member of the Linnet choir can read music. Iker can read music. So Iker is a member of the choir.`],
      a:4, why:`Parallel flaw. The original takes "all from the stand are pesticide free" and reasons backward from pesticide free to from the stand. The choir argument does the same: all members read music, Iker reads music, so he's a member. The member who reads music and the paid singer who isn't a member are valid arguments, the most members argument is a reasonable probability claim, and the one about a nonmember commits a different flaw, reasoning from not a member to can't read.`, task:`LR15`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`After the Garnet Grill began sending text reminders to diners with reservations, the share of diners who failed to show up fell from one in five to one in twenty. Yet the restaurant now serves fewer dinners on a typical night than it did before the reminders began.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?`,
      opts:[`Diners who get a reminder are somewhat more likely to order dessert and coffee afterward.`,`Several other restaurants nearby also began sending text reminders that same year.`,`It used to overbook to cover no shows, and stopped once the reminders began.`,`Most of the restaurant's diners make their reservations at least a full day ahead.`,`The restaurant raised its prices slightly the spring before the reminders began.`],
      a:2, why:`Resolve the paradox. If the restaurant used to overbook to cover no shows and stopped, it now books fewer diners, so more of them showing up can still mean fewer dinners. Dessert orders and booking habits don't explain the drop, other restaurants' reminders don't explain this one's numbers, and a price rise before the change doesn't explain a change after it.`, task:`LR18`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`City planner: The new bus line will reduce car traffic downtown. Surveys show that about thirty percent of downtown commuters would ride a bus that stopped within a few blocks of their homes, and the new line will stop within a few blocks of the homes of many of them.<br><br>The planner's argument depends on which one of the following assumptions?`,
      opts:[`At least some of the commuters who say they'd ride such a bus now travel downtown by car.`,`Most downtown commuters would rather ride a bus than a train if they could choose.`,`The new bus line will run more often than any of the city's existing lines.`,`Car traffic downtown is worse than it is in any other part of the city.`,`Every commuter who was surveyed lives within a few blocks of the new line.`],
      a:0, why:`Necessary assumption. If none of the would be riders now travel by car, moving them onto a bus takes no cars off the road. Deny it and the argument collapses. A preference over trains, frequency and the comparison with other areas aren't needed, and "every commuter" is far more than the argument needs.`, task:`LR04`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`STAT`]},
    {q:`The authors of the report would most likely agree with which one of the following statements?`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`Unknown Male 1 is the man who struck Mr. Hensley at the curb.`,`Luis Ruiz's DNA was left during a struggle in the parking lot behind the bar.`,`Teo Ruiz was not present when Mr. Hensley was injured.`,`The DNA under the left hand was most likely left there by hospital staff.`,`The results alone don't show when Unknown Male 1's DNA got under the nails.`],
      a:4, why:`Would agree. The report's last lines say its results identify whose DNA is present and don't establish when or by what kind of contact it was deposited. It names no attacker, says nothing about where Luis's DNA was left, excludes Teo only as a contributor, and offers no theory about the hospital.`, task:`RC04`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`The report's statement that no database match was found for Unknown Male 1 is most analogous to which one of the following?`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`A thermometer reading that shows no fever, which rules out every kind of infection.`,`A blank page in a shop's ledger, which proves the shop made no sales at all that day.`,`A fingerprint on a window that matches a known burglar, which shows who touched the glass there.`,`A catalog search that finds no record of a title, which shows only that it isn't listed.`,`A missing person found safe at home, which closes the search for good.`],
      a:3, why:`Analogy. No match means the profile isn't in the databases searched, not that the man is unknowable or doesn't exist, just as a title missing from one catalog may still exist. The thermometer and ledger overclaim from an absence, the fingerprint is a match, not a miss, and the found person is an outcome, not a search result.`, task:`RC10`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Which one of the following most accurately describes the relationship between the two passages?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`Passage B rejects the findings reported in Passage A as the product of flawed and outdated laboratory methods.`,`Passage B grants that memory can be altered afterward and argues that a proper first test still tells you something.`,`Passage A answers in advance an objection that Passage B raises against research on eyewitness memory.`,`Passage B applies the methods of the experiments in Passage A to the study of police lineups.`,`Both passages argue that courts should give no weight at all to a witness's confidence in an identification at trial.`],
      a:1, why:`Comparative passages. Passage B says its authors didn't deny that memory can be contaminated, then argues that a first test under pristine conditions is informative. It narrows the lesson drawn from work like A's without rejecting A. A doesn't answer B, B doesn't run A's experiments, and B argues that confidence can deserve weight.`, task:`RC14`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`The authors of both passages would most likely agree that`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`a witness's confidence at trial is a reliable sign that the witness is accurate`,`memory for numbers, such as speed, is sturdier than memory for details`,`police lineups are the leading cause of mistaken convictions`,`courts should no longer allow eyewitnesses to testify at trial`,`what happens to a witness after an event can change what the witness reports`],
      a:4, why:`Comparative passages. Passage A shows a later question changing what witnesses report. Passage B grants that memory can be contaminated and limits its claims to a first test for that reason. Neither vouches for confidence at trial, compares numbers with details, ranks the causes of wrongful convictions, or would bar eyewitnesses.`, task:`RC14`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Which one of the following is most analogous to the second experiment described in Passage A?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`Diners told a soup is fiery are likelier a week later to recall chili peppers in it, though it had none.`,`Diners who are hungry rate the same soup more highly than diners who have just eaten a meal.`,`A cook who tastes the same soup twice in one evening gives it exactly the same score both times.`,`Diners asked to describe a soup in their own words use more words than diners who are given a list of words.`,`A food critic changes her review of a soup after learning which famous chef cooked it.`],
      a:0, why:`Analogy. In the experiment a word in a question led people, a week later, to remember a detail that wasn't there. The soup case has the same bones: a word, a delay, a remembered detail that never existed. Hunger changes a rating, not a memory, the cook's scores don't change, the description task is about wordiness, and the critic changes a judgment, not a recollection.`, task:`RC10`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Which one of the following, if true, would most strengthen the argument made in Passage B?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`Many witnesses grow more confident in their identifications in the months between the lineup and the trial.`,`Mock crime studies more often use college students as witnesses than members of the general public.`,`One department's records show confident first picks from fair lineups were confirmed far more often than unsure ones.`,`Some police departments still use lineups in which the officer running them knows which of the people is the suspect.`,`Witnesses who see a suspect's photograph in the news often change their accounts of the crime afterward.`],
      a:2, why:`Strengthen. Passage B claims that under proper conditions high confidence first identifications tend to be accurate and low confidence ones don't. Field records showing exactly that pattern support it. Confidence growing before trial and photographs in the news are about contamination B already grants, the makeup of mock crime samples raises a doubt, and departments using poor lineups describe the problem without supporting the claim.`, task:`RC13`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `It was ten to five when Meredith sat down. Esquibel took his glasses off and looked at nothing for a while, longer than Dani expected, and then he said he was taking it under advisement and both sides would get his ruling in writing, and thanked them, and the clerk said all rise, and everybody did.`,
    `The deputy came for her father at the side door. He stood, and he looked at the front row where Teo was for as long as the deputy let him, which wasn't long. Teo lifted one hand off his knee and put it back. Then the door shut.`,
    `In the hall the light came in sideways through the tall windows, and people stood around in it with nowhere to put their hands. Celeste Abeyta told Teo he'd done fine and shook his hand. Bianca had driven down at noon and was at the far end by the drinking fountain with her keys out. Ana was already on the phone with somebody, a pencil behind her ear.`,
    `Shannon Hensley came out of the courtroom last, alone, with her purse under her arm. She stopped across the hall from Dani, maybe ten feet off, and looked at her. She looked for a long time. She didn't say anything at all.`,
    `Then she went down the stairs instead of waiting on the elevator, with one hand on the rail. Dani stood where she was, with her bag on her shoulder and the prep book in it, and the envelope in the back of the book. MAYBE HE DID IT, across the top. Her capitals. December. Not crossed out.`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch11.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`The Last Move`
});
